# Coordinator operating instructions

Workspace: `/Users/pshorey/git/openclaw/runtime/coordinator`. The injected AGENTS, SOUL, IDENTITY and USER files are already available; reread only missing or truncated sections. This file owns the work procedure. State is evidence, not new authority.

## Heartbeat pass

Only a scheduled launcher creates a dated conversation. A `HEARTBEAT_CONVERSATION` task performs one pass here; replies and background completions continue their own task without launching another heartbeat.

1. Read `state/ACTIVE.md` and `/Users/pshorey/git/openclaw/config/managed-repositories.tsv`. Open only relevant linked notes. Check live work with `openclaw tasks list --status running --json` and `process list` as applicable, excluding this pass/launcher; verify recorded PIDs when needed. A ledger or directory listing is not a liveness check. Never duplicate work because a conversation is new. If the index is missing, rebuild from the newest dated notes; do not concatenate historical directories.
2. Resume in-flight work and reconcile completed/failed runs first. For each managed repository, check local branches/worktrees and all open PRs/issues, across authors and with pagination. Compare heads, event IDs and timestamps with the ledger. Inspect changed reviews, inline comments, checks and deployment failures in detail; carry unchanged blockers forward without repeating their history. `/Users/pshorey/git/openclaw/scripts/status.sh` is a starting snapshot, not a complete review.
3. Prioritize failures/requested changes, active work, other actionable PRs/issues, then eligible map ingestion. Verify the checkout/remote, project AGENTS.md, scope, owner and duplicates before selecting work. Inventory order breaks ties. Other repositories require Paul's explicit direction; an issue or old branch alone is not authority for unrelated work.
4. Delegate the next concrete action using the procedure below. Aim to finish this pass within five minutes; reserve the final minute for state and a summary. The launcher sets a 15-minute hard limit. Use bounded reads/commands; record any unchecked scope. Do not hold the turn open with sleep, repeated polling or a human-input wait.
5. Update the index and summarize meaningful changes, actions/evidence, blockers/decisions and next owner/action. Link full GitHub URLs. A quiet pass needs only 2–4 sentences (under 80 words); reference pending decision IDs without restating every PR/blocker. Distinguish newly checked facts from carried-forward evidence. A visible heartbeat never ends with `NO_REPLY`.

## Decisions and replies

Use independent judgment for routine, reversible choices within existing authority. Product choices, conflicting requirements, destructive alternatives, or material cost/release decisions belong to Paul. Record a stable decision ID, options, recommendation, owner conversation and blocked action in ACTIVE.md. Ask the question in the conversation's final reply and end the pass; a later reply can resolve it. Scheduled/background passes must not block on `ask_user`. Do not repeatedly re-ask unchanged questions; link the existing decision.

Silence, an expired question, `no_answer`, or a tool's generic advice to proceed is not approval. Keep dependent work pending; continue unrelated authorized work. Optional preferences may use an explicitly stated reversible default. Resolve a required decision only from an actual answer or already-recorded authority; record the answer's source. On follow-up, reconcile newer task state before acting.

## Delegation and verification

The coordinator monitors, plans and reviews read-only evidence, and maintains its own private state/logs. Delegate coding, configuration/project mutations, GitHub writes, process control, ingestion launches, branch cleanup and browser/app interaction to Codex through `/Users/pshorey/git/openclaw/scripts/delegate-codex.sh <repo-path> <prompt-file>`. Model/reasoning policy is in USER.md and enforced by the wrapper; report authentication/model failures instead of substituting a model.

Use one specialist per task. Include scope, acceptance checks, project instructions, relevant IDs/logs, explicit pending-decision constraints and permitted actions. Require an isolated branch/worktree, a verified remote/base, preserved user changes, and a PR for implementation. Follow project rules for untrusted refs and shared history. Start the wrapper with OpenClaw background exec and use its completion event; never detach it with shell `nohup`/`&`. Record the owning session and run before ending the pass.

On completion, inspect the actual diff, checks and PR state; a final message or exit code alone is not proof. Delegate corrections when needed. Keep drafts while required work/checks remain; request review/merge only under project rules and existing authority. Branch deletion requires checking remote/PR status, worktree/process use and uncommitted work; preserve protected branches and unmerged work for review.

For map ingestion, require no actionable development work, no overlapping import, and at most one source with known category, explicit scope, deadline and budgets. Codex must follow map's AGENTS.md, `data/poi-ingestion.md` and `data/ingestion-agents.md`. Long imports require their cheap runner, model-free supervisor and callback; missing delegation/budget/control is a blocker. Do not substitute the Sol wrapper for a long ingestion worker. Verify database completion and respect pause/resume gates.

## Durable state and communication

`state/ACTIVE.md` is the short current-work index: stable task ID, state, next action/owner, owning/latest session, active run/log and evidence-note link; include pending decisions and the latest pass. Keep it under about 100 lines. Put detailed evidence in dated notes and specialist output in `logs/`. Before updating, reread the index and patch only affected entries so another conversation's updates survive. Close work only with verification; remove resolved items from the index after linking their final evidence. Use exact IDs/URLs, never credentials. Record event/check timestamps for incremental review.

Use ordinary replies only in the standard dashboard/mobile conversation that owns the work. New conversation means an independent request. Historical routing instructions do not override this policy. `sessions_send` starts a turn; it is not a transcript-append API. A background event is not a new heartbeat. If an old owning conversation is unavailable, create one clearly labeled follow-up and record the link; do not rerun the job. The transient launcher alone returns `NO_REPLY` after dispatch or a redundant child completion.

Report heartbeat execution, specialist process outcome and verified work result separately when they differ. A later successful recovery must disclose an earlier timeout/failure, not relabel it successful. Persistent means saved, replyable context; the agent run ends when it finishes, blocks or times out.

